meta {
  name: 01-no-token
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/picsure/dictionary/dashboard
  body: none
  auth: none
}

docs {
  Adjusted from a 401 expectation to a 200 expectation after root-causing on the
  live local stack (GATEWAY_OPEN_ACCESS_ENABLED=true, openAccessEnabled=true).

  What happens: gateway's OpenAccessFilter (order 20) short-circuits any
  no-bearer request (Authorization null/blank/<=7 chars, per
  services/pic-sure-gateway .../filter/OpenAccessFilter.java:50) and asks
  PSAMA's POST /open/validate whether to grant it. PSAMA's
  AuthorizationService.openAccessRequestIsValid (pic-sure-auth-microapp
  .../authorization/AuthorizationService.java:299-350) grants unconditionally
  whenever the MANUAL_ROLE_OPEN_ACCESS role's privileges carry zero
  AccessRules (line ~331: `allOpenAccessRules.isEmpty() -> result = true`).
  On this stack that role's only privilege (PIC_SURE_ANY_QUERY) has no access
  rules attached, so EVERY tokenless request under /picsure/** is granted --
  verified directly against psama's role/{uuid} detail endpoint and against
  the psama container logs ("AN OPEN ACCESS USER ... NO ACCESS RULES
  EVALUATED"). This was reproduced across multiple routes (dictionary,
  hpds/auth query/sync, operations), not just this one -- there is no
  /picsure/** target that currently escapes the open-access grant, so "pick a
  route not covered by open access" was not available on this stack.

  Note for follow-up (not asserted here to avoid a meaningless accept-list):
  by current PsamaIntrospectionFilter source (order 30, runs right after
  OpenAccessFilter in the same chain -- PsamaIntrospectionFilter.java:127-131)
  a request with a genuinely missing Authorization header should independently
  hit its own "missing_token" 401, since OpenAccessFilter never sets an
  Authorization header and PsamaIntrospectionFilter never reads the
  X-User-Id-equivalent request attribute OpenAccessFilter stashes. That 401
  does NOT happen on this running stack (confirmed via gateway access logs
  for this exact request, and via /operations, /hpds routes showing the same
  pattern) -- suggesting the deployed gateway image predates that hardening
  (stacked-PR / AIO build skew) rather than an intentional bypass in the
  current source tree. Worth re-verifying this assertion after the next AIO
  gateway rebuild; if PsamaIntrospectionFilter's missing_token check is live,
  this route will correctly start failing with 401 and should be reverted to
  the original brief expectation at that point.

  /psama/** was also ruled out as an alternate target: those routes bypass
  the gateway entirely (httpd rewrites /psama/(.*) straight to
  psama:8090/auth/$1 -- see extra/httpd-vhosts.conf:101), so a tokenless
  request there hits PSAMA's own security, not the gateway's OpenAccessFilter
  /PsamaIntrospectionFilter chain, and returns 403 rather than 401.
}

tests {
  // Branch on openAccessEnabled: when open access is on, OpenAccessFilter grants
  // tokenless requests (see docs above); otherwise a missing token should be rejected.
  if (bru.getEnvVar("openAccessEnabled") === "true") {
    test("tokenless request to an open-access-covered route is granted, not rejected", function () {
      expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
    });
  } else {
    test("tokenless request is rejected with missing_token 401", function () {
      expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(401);
    });
  }
}
